WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:07.770
For a long time, if you went to the library
in Wilmington, North Carolina, there was

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.070
one thing you weren’t allowed to research.

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:11.360
“We were refused.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.820
We were rejected by the librarian.”

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.550
“When I asked about or inquired about 1898
they wanted to know why.”

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:22.340
“I was told that yes they had something
but they kept it under lock and key."

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.099
The story of Wilmington in 1898 still isn't
widely known.

00:00:27.099 --> 00:00:31.140
“What happened here — on what’s now
just this empty patch of grass would radically

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:34.870
change racial politics in North Carolina.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:41.359
This is the story of an American election, but
also of something we don't usually find in

00:00:41.359 --> 00:00:43.150
American history.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:47.900
The violent overthrow of a democratically
elected government.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:03.870
In the late 1800s, Wilmington, North Carolina
was the state’s largest city.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.680
It had a majority black population, and historians
today

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.080
describe it as a rarity in the post-Civil War American South.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:17.820
“Wilmington prior to November, 1898, was
what the New South could be at the cusp of

00:01:17.831 --> 00:01:19.020
the 20th century.”

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.150
“There was an unusual degree of black prosperity.”

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:30.040
In Wilmington there were successful black
entrepreneurs, doctors, teachers -- but also

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.280
black elected officials.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.240
And for a time, that was true throughout the
State.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:41.340
Take a look at the politicians on this poster
of the 1889 North Carolina House of Representatives.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.430
Here at the bottom — are black Republican
representatives — some from Wilmington.

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:54.700
North Carolina also sent four black Republicans
to the US Congress between 1875 and 1899.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:01.280
The Democratic and Republican parties of 1898
in many ways occupied opposite parts of the

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.920
political spectrum than they do today.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.890
“Most African-Americans were voting for
the Republican Party.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:10.740
And the Democratic Party was white voters
almost exclusively.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:16.989
White supremacy was the central focus of the
platform for the Democratic Party.”

00:02:16.989 --> 00:02:22.510
Republicans in North Carolina were successful
in part because of a 3rd party called the

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:27.439
"Populist" party, made up of mostly white
farmers fed up with the tough economic times.

00:02:27.439 --> 00:02:34.390
North Carolina Populists joined up with Republicans
to form what they called the Fusion Party.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:42.110
And in the elections of 1894 and 1896, the
Fusion party defeated the Democrats in sweeping

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:44.030
victories statewide.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:48.740
That meant North Carolina now had a government
that shared power between black and white

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:52.090
politicians, including a newly elected Republican
governor.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:57.460
Together, they moved towards reforms that
would favor black Americans and working-class whites.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:03.709
“This was something that the Democratic
Party folks were simply not going to accept.”

00:03:03.709 --> 00:03:09.840
A multiracial government wasn’t just a disappointment
for Democrats. It was more like a humiliation.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.469
They needed a plan to take back control of
the state in the next election.

00:03:14.469 --> 00:03:20.040
So party leaders, like Furnifold Simmons,
future US Senator; Charles Aycock, future

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:26.209
North Carolina Governor; and this man, Alfred
Moore Wadell, came up with one:

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:33.080
To beat the Fusion Party by luring white Populist
voters away from their alliance with Black voters.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:39.060
Wilmington, with a large black population
and a local Fusion government in power — would

00:03:39.079 --> 00:03:41.889
be a focus of their campaign.

00:03:41.889 --> 00:03:47.069
The state Democratic state party handbook
for 1898 laid out their goal: consolidate

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:50.859
the white vote by stoking white anger and
resentment.

00:03:50.859 --> 00:03:57.379
It said: “this is a white man’s country
and white men must control and govern it.”

00:03:57.379 --> 00:04:00.340
Their most effective tool was the media.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:04.799
One of North Carolina’s biggest newspapers
was a Democratic Party mouthpiece.

00:04:04.799 --> 00:04:08.560
It ran racist political cartoons throughout
1898.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.700
“Not everybody was literate in 1898.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:17.830
But to see a political cartoon of the type
that &nbsp;ran you may not be able to read it

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:19.819
but you know exactly what it means.”

00:04:19.819 --> 00:04:25.050
Many of the cartoons were centered on the
threat of “Negro domination”...even though

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.830
the Fusion government was mostly white.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:30.040
They also played up another fear.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:34.360
“Black men threatening white women became
a theme.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:39.000
White men need to do all that they can to
protect white womanhood.”

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:44.419
This was all part of North Carolina democratic
strategy, but it echoed the national racist

00:04:44.419 --> 00:04:46.879
rhetoric of the time.

00:04:46.879 --> 00:04:51.970
In one speech that Democrats printed in a
Wilmington paper — a prominent Georgia writer

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:54.100
named Rebecca Felton said:

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:59.310
“If it takes lynching a black man a day
to protect white womanhood ‘I say lynch".

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:05.199
Her speech prompted a Wilmington black man
named Alex Manly, owner of the black-run,

00:05:05.199 --> 00:05:08.320
Daily Record newspaper, to respond with
a column.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.979
He made a simple observation, that at the
time, was shocking.

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:21.349
“That white women who had liaisons with
black men did so voluntarily and enthusiastically.”

00:05:21.349 --> 00:05:27.559
Manly wrote “Every Negro lynched is called
a "big burly, black brute", when in fact many

00:05:27.559 --> 00:05:31.840
were sufficiently attractive for white
girls to fall in love with them.”

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:37.090
“Manly pretty much said in a nutshell: sometimes
white women choose to be with black men.”

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:41.520
Manly’s editorial became another tool for
Democrats.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:47.430
Newspapers reprinted it, called it “A Horrid
Slander”, and ran comments about it on a

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:48.970
daily basis.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:54.520
It was just a few months before the election,
and white voters were angry.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:06:02.879
“By the time the election rolls around on
November 8th black voters, Republican voters

00:06:02.879 --> 00:06:04.750
had been thoroughly intimidated here.”

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:10.379
By all accounts, the elections of 1898 were
a sham.

00:06:10.379 --> 00:06:14.699
The Democratic party had a paramilitary group
called the Red Shirts.

00:06:14.699 --> 00:06:17.710
They attacked and blocked black residents
from voting.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:22.560
At a rally just before the election, Alfred
Moore Waddell provoked the crowds.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:28.190
He said “negro office-holding ought at once,
and forever be brought to an end. Even

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:33.720
if we have to choke the current of the
Cape Fear River with carcasses.”

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.060
The votes were counted, and the Democrats
won.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:41.590
“Democratic candidates won every seat they
had a candidate up for election in.”

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:46.509
But some local Fusionist politicians remained
in power, because their seats hadn’t

00:06:46.509 --> 00:06:50.889
been up for re-election like the white Republican
mayor and the board of aldermen.

00:06:50.889 --> 00:06:55.830
And and of course the election did nothing
to undo the economic power black folks held

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:57.120
in the city.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.630
The Democrats had won the election, but their
goal of total white supremacist control remained

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:03.759
out of reach.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:07.180
“And so they engineered what was essentially
a coup d'etat.”

00:07:09.009 --> 00:07:14.280
The day after the election, at a gathering
for white men in Wilmington, the Democrats

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.729
unveiled a document they called the “White
Declaration of Independence.”

00:07:18.729 --> 00:07:21.669
It contained an ultimatum.

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:27.270
Cynthia Brown, whose descendants were in Wilmington
back in 1898, is a historian at her church,

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:31.790
where there’s a preserved copy of the declaration
from the next day’s newspaper.

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:37.710
“We will no longer be ruled and will never
again be ruled by men of African origin.”

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:40.080
They would strip black men of voting rights.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.140
They would give “white men a large part
of the employment heretofore given to” black men.

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:47.560
And as for Alex Manly...

00:07:47.569 --> 00:07:51.990
“We demand that he leave this City forever
within twenty-four hours.”

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:56.259
The next morning, hundreds of white men marched

00:07:56.259 --> 00:07:59.280
to the offices of the Daily Record.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:02.530
Manly was gone -- he had fled to save his
own life.

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:07.349
They set the Daily Record building on fire.

00:08:07.349 --> 00:08:10.180
This is where it once stood.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:15.990
“Once the White leadership destroyed Alex
Manly's printing press they destroyed one

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:20.350
way in which the African-American community
in Wilmington could organize itself and keep

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:21.860
itself informed.”

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:26.759
At City Hall — the mayor and board of aldermen
were forced out.

00:08:26.759 --> 00:08:31.439
“There's two hundred armed men in City Hall
at the time.

00:08:31.439 --> 00:08:37.530
They didn't do it of their own free will and
as they resigned a new member selected by

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:40.360
the Democratic Party was voted into office.”

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:45.620
Waddell — who once threatened to fill the
Cape Fear River with black bodies — was

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:48.160
the new mayor of Wilmington.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:55.290
Meanwhile, the mob had grown to about 2,000
men, and the violence spilled into the streets.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:09:00.279
In these photos, exes mark where the first
black residents were killed.

00:09:00.279 --> 00:09:05.410
“The stories are that they were dumped into
the river.

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:09.190
And there are varying stories about how many
people were killed.”

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:14.440
“To me, I see 40 to 60 clearly as fatalities
as a result of the violence.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.860
But I think it was higher.”

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:23.760
Many black residents hid for days in the swamps,
and the wooded cemeteries in the city — including

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.410
Cynthia’s great grandmother.

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:33.279
And thousands of other residents fled Wilmington,
never to return.

00:09:33.279 --> 00:09:39.820
Shortly afterward, democrats printed booklets
celebrating a glorious victory...and in the

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:43.019
newspapers, depicted black residents as the
instigators.

00:09:43.019 --> 00:09:49.300
“This image is a gross misrepresentation
of what actually happened.

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:56.720
You know what you see are black men with guns
not white men with machine guns.”

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:00.420
The city never regained its black majority
population.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:07.350
Jim Crow laws, like literacy tests and poll
taxes that prevented black people from voting

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:13.790
were immediately enacted and Wilmington’s
spirit of black opportunity was crushed.

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:17.510
Black political representation in the state
was over.

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:24.300
It would be 90 years until North Carolina
elected its next black Congress member.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:30.770
“Wilmington did a really great job of covering
up a very dark past for a very long time.”

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:34.260
“Over the years, the textbooks on North
Carolina’s state history have struggled

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:38.170
to accurately describe what happened in 1898.

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:43.450
This book from 1933 calls what happened “unfortunate
for both races.”

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:46.900
And this one from 1978 doesn’t have that
much more detail.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:53.160
But they both praise Charles Aycock, one of
the perpetrators of the riot, as a man with

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:59.840
‘a kind heart’ and that in fact he was
one of ‘the best friends that the colored people had in the state.'

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:06.440
It’s a legacy that North Carolina has yet
to fully undo.

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:11.790
The names of the perpetrators are on Wilmington's
school buildings and city parks.

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:18.360
But the legacy is also bigger than those names.

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:24.880
Turn on the news, and the state's long history
of political suppression echoes.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:28.620
“We turn to a strict new voter ID law in
North Carolina.”

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:32.139
“Racially gerrymandering and a push for
new voting maps.”

00:11:32.139 --> 00:11:37.430
“The court says the Republican-led legislature
redrew Congressional districts along racial

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:39.329
lines, violating the Constitution.”

00:11:39.329 --> 00:11:44.000
“There is a tremendous amount of intimidation
that is still felt by the black community.”

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:48.220
“It doesn't have to be mass mayhem and violence
in the streets.”

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:54.220
“The strategy shifts towards designing state
laws in such a way that you could exclude

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:57.340
blacks from voter participation.”

00:11:57.340 --> 00:12:02.160
“The subliminal pursuit of continuing
the White declaration of independence.”

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:06.650
“And if you don't see it for what it really
is, it can happen all over again.”

